onsistent  everywhere in the country 58 prefer the  measures to be  regionally variable  so nina any indication that the  preferences expressed breakdown  along party lines for example you know  how is this looking  oh yes this was extremely clear in this  poll the polls always ask for party  affiliations  which party do you support and so we do  have a very clear picture  supporters of five out of the seven  parties seem to be clearly split in two  when it comes to whether to stick to the  current restrictions or to allow for  gradually  gradual opening if you take angela  merkel's conservatives for example  43 percent want restrictions to stay in  place  as they are versus 52 who say that there  needs to be some careful opening and  that is of course  very close 43 versus 52's angela merkel  is in a dilemma with her own party  and the same is true for the social  democrats the greens the left party  followers  but then you've got the far-right afd  and the business-friendly fdp  party where the picture is a completely  different one where there is an  overwhelming desire to open up or open  up gradually and that is  of course not surprising because the fdp  is the party for germany's  middle stand for hotel owners etc where  many firms are struggling to survive  absolutely and you know we're seeing  some of those struggles actually playing  out in the approval ratings  uh we mentioned earlier that approval  ratings particularly for chancellor  merkel  for her government have been falling  just 50 percent of those polled say that  they were satisfied  with the policies of merkel's coalition  49  say they were not satisfied  so nina ultimately is there a chance  that this pandemic could be ruining  angela merkel's reputation  now at the end of her political career  well yeah she's not going to stand again  as candidate for  the chancellorship because germans of  course go to the polls in september  but if you look at her approval ratings  at the beginning of the crisis  and where we are now they of course they  have somewhat gone down  if you compare it to the first wave when  they were as high as they hadn't been  for five years because she was seen as  somebody who can master crises  and that has to do now the fact that  they've gone down with the sluggish  start of the vaccination campaign here  the tug of war  with and between the 16 state leaders  and the performance of her cabinet her  health minister her  economics minister who've also had  massive slums in  their approval ratings but angela  merkel's approval ratings are still  higher than they were in the two years  leading up to the start of this crisis  so yes she's not as popular as she was  during the first wave  but 64 approval still makes her  germany's most popular politician  at the moment dw political correspondent  nina jose  reporting from berlin and dw political  correspondent simon young  talked through germany's latest steps in  combating the virus with  carl lauterbach he's an epidemiologist  and a member of parliament who has  become known as a voice  of caution is chancellor merkel  leading germany in the right direction  in this pandemic  this is a very general question and i'm  not giving  um i'm not in a position basically  to uh to provide grades for her  performance that is not what i would  like to do  i think that the decisions that we have  come upon yesterday  have a couple of very strong points for  example to show  to change the vaccination strategy  so that we have a further delay between  the first and the second vaccination so  that we can  provide more all elderly people with  first vaccination  i also believe it is correct to use the  astrazeneca  vaccination for the elderly and also  believes that it is correct to use a  test  national testing strategy an  evidence-based  national testing strategy to let's say  work against a third wave which we are  currently  encountering but a general position  about what she has  what merkel has done and not what we  currently do is basically a  common performance by the governors of  the states and  the federal government and even  parliament  the chancellor talks about an emergency  break if infections go  back up you don't believe in the  emergency brake i believe in the  emergency brake  once we are up to let's say an incidence  rate of 100  per 100 000 inhabitants per week  we will come back to the lockdown that  we currently have  so that is the emergency break and i do  not only believe that this is an  emergency break i also believe we will  have to use it  right you've tweeted uh that the  strategy will cost us a lot of covid  cases and it won't help the economy  aren't you being too pessimistic  i don't think i'm too pessimistic what's  going to happen we will have  increasing case numbers because we are  in a third wave and the decisions from  yesterday  will not help to curtail the third wave  and at the same time we will not be able  to help the economy because we cannot  open restaurants or bars or anything  because when there is an  increase in caseload such openings will  not happen  so you agree with the bavarian leader  marco cerda who says that  our hearts tell us to open up but common  sense says  be cautious don't people need to have a  perspective  of how lockdown is going to end i do  think we have a perspective so  perspective is here vaccination  for everyone who is prepared to get  vaccinated to speed up the vaccination  project and we have come  up with important decisions with respect  to that  yesterday very important decisions and  to use  entity and testing self-testing as a  bridge technology so that is a  perspective  right the strategy is based on  testing as you say are there enough  tests self-tests and quick tests  available  in germany at least we do know that  enough tests could  be produced in germany we now make have  to make sure we cannot  allow a mistake here we have to make  sure that the tests that  can be produced in germany will be  produced and will also be used in  germany so that is very important  you've made a name as a critic of  rapid easing in this pandemic and  a warning voice what do you think is the  most important thing that the government  should do  going forward we should make it very  clear that a third wave is not a minor  problem that this is not a small wave  before everyone gets the vaccination  but we in order to let's see if the risk  groups vaccinated  have another couple of months to go that  is a very long stretch  in order to go through a third wave  so we have to be very very careful to  provide the testing strategy and you  also have to make the population aware  that further lockdowns despite the fact  that everyone hates them may be  necessary  can you predict when lockdowns will  finally end in germany  i think that lockdowns will end in  germany once we have let's say  vaccinated the majority of the  population professor lutebeck thank you  very much  you
 

